          "text": "{\"relation\":\"REFINED\",\"consensus_summary\":\"Clean/unclean primarily symbolizes moral and spiritual purity versus sinful defilement; cleansing signifies forgiveness, sanctification, and restored fitness for God's presence, while ritual status is acknowledged as a contextual alternative.\",\"book_summary\":\"Cleanliness is freedom from physical, ceremonial, or moral defilement and consequent fitness for permitted contact, approach, or use. Uncleanness need not involve guilt, and cleanliness is distinguished from sanctification.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"Cleanliness is chiefly moral-spiritual purity before God, and cleansing is removal of sinful defilement, often associated with forgiveness and sanctification.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"Cleanliness is the broader condition of being free from defilement and fit for permitted relations or use; cleansing removes defilement, whether physical, ceremonial, or moral.\",\"core_relation\":\"BOOK_BROADENS\",\"extension_relation\":\"BOOK_SUPERSET\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"The book includes nonmoral physical and ceremonial states as genuine cases of uncleanness and cleansing, not merely obsolete rites or symbols of sin. It also declines to identify cleanliness simply with holiness or sanctification.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"The book rejects any universal collapse of uncleanness into moral guilt or of cleansing into sanctification. It does not reject the consensus's moral cases, but treats them as one subset of a wider category.\",\"rationale\":\"The book retains the consensus's basic defilement-and-removal category and expressly includes cleansing from sin, so it does not replace the central referent. It materially broadens the extension to childbirth, sexual emission, corpse contact, and other guiltless ceremonial or physical conditions, while sharpening the distinction between clean and holy. This is a boundary and classification refinement rather than a divergent identification; moreover, the consensus responses themselves recognize ritual, nonmoral status as a common alternative.\",\"citation_support\":\"STRONG\",\"strongest_objection\":\"Because the consensus responses explicitly acknowledge ritual impurity as a common or even dominant contextual meaning, the book could be viewed as matching the consensus taken as a whole. However, it materially revises the stated primary identification by making nonmoral ceremonial cases integral to the definition rather than secondary qualifications.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
        }
